WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.450
Narrator: As the coronavirus pandemic

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:04.680
continues to escalate,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.010
we are flooded with images
that may be contributing

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.500
to our anxiety.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.190
We watch as cities around
the world are disinfected

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.610
and get constant updates
on the number of cases

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.070
near and far.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.090
When we get away from the TV and internet,

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.560
there isn't much relief.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.290
We are faced with closed
offices and empty store shelves.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.940
We spoke to a therapist to find out

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.123
how to deal with coronavirus anxiety.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.710
So can you talk to me about

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.740
what is at the core of our anxiety

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.070
and how does this anxiety serve us?

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.368
So, anxiety really is healthy
and evolutionary, right?

00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:41.199
I mean, it is there to
say you need to prepare,

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:43.860
you need to watch out,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.600
and anxiety really precedes action.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.813
You know, we would see a tiger
on the savanna or whatever,

00:00:48.813 --> 00:00:50.660
or we'd have a lack of food,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.500
and anxiety showed up to
make sure that we acted

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.327
and protected ourselves from
whatever that threat was.

00:00:57.327 --> 00:01:01.313
Now in this instance, it's
an interesting situation

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:05.062
because we don't have enough information

00:01:05.062 --> 00:01:08.890
to really be able to assess
how productive our anxiety is.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.210
We want to find somewhere
between alarmism and nonchalance,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.340
and we don't know where that is

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.713
because we don't have enough information

00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:19.260
and there is a large
degree of misinformation.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.776
So this is all to say that the
anxiety that we're feeling,

00:01:21.776 --> 00:01:24.610
a certain degree of it
actually is really helpful.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:25.850
One of the things we tend to do,

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:27.550
with any uncomfortable emotion,

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.400
whether it's anxiety or
sadness or loneliness,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.480
is we judge ourselves

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.030
and then we end up feeling
anxiety for feeling anxious,

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.110
or ashamed for feeling
anxious or depressed

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.170
or whatever it is.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.830
So in this case,

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.230
we want to be able to sort of take pause,

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.050
recognize that we're feeling anxious

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.180
and make some space for that anxiety.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.370
And actually practice what
we call self-compassion

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:49.400
in response to that.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.637
Those are real, valid, warranted fears.

00:01:52.637 --> 00:01:56.399
Now the challenge is a
lot of people, again,

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:58.580
they don't actually go so far

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.510
as to understand what their
anxiety is telling them.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.100
They just sort of
catastrophize and ruminate.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.660
And so what we want people to do

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.760
is to be able to recognize
the anxiety they're feeling

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.983
and then ask themselves,
what's anxiety telling me?

00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:15.110
And anxiety is essentially
correlated with uncertainty.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.010
OK, what am I most scared about?

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:17.860
Is it my health?

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.490
Is it my parents' or a loved one's health?

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:21.580
Is it my job?

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.200
You know, is it the markets?

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.107
And then start to really understand,

00:02:25.107 --> 00:02:26.547
what do I need right now?

00:02:26.547 --> 00:02:29.027
Now, in terms of dealing with uncertainty

00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:31.880
and thus lowering our anxiety,

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.480
there are two ways of coming at this:

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.217
One, we want to be able to
find certainty where we can.

00:02:36.217 --> 00:02:39.645
I would encourage people to
find a reputable media source,

00:02:39.645 --> 00:02:44.115
check that not constantly or nonstop,

00:02:44.115 --> 00:02:46.800
because you will just fuel the anxiety

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.000
and it will just frankly be exhausting

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.500
and incredibly distressful.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.891
So find your reputable
news source and check it -

00:02:52.891 --> 00:02:54.860
give yourself a number of times a day

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.190
that you might check it,

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.343
and also playing out different scenarios,

00:02:58.343 --> 00:03:01.951
thinking OK, if I want to
feel a level of preparedness

00:03:01.951 --> 00:03:04.533
in this situation, what
seems realistic, in terms,

00:03:04.533 --> 00:03:06.340
of responsible preparation?

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.667
Yes, do I want to go out and
but some extra toilet paper

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:12.387
and non-perishables,
or water, or whatever.

00:03:12.387 --> 00:03:14.334
If that seems to serve you

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:16.263
and help lower you
anxiety in the situation

00:03:16.263 --> 00:03:17.970
then by all means do it.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.423
You know, another thing we can
do to feel a sense of control

00:03:20.423 --> 00:03:22.743
and find more certainty around this,

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:26.310
is, OK, are you washing your hands?

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.570
Are you practicing social distancing?

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:32.340
Are you doing what you can
to support your immune system

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.640
in the event that you are exposed?

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.900
And that means getting enough sleep,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.860
making sure that you're
nourishing your body.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.940
Then when it comes to
coping with uncertainty

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.481
and sitting with uncertainty,

00:03:42.481 --> 00:03:45.750
self-care of course is super important.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.110
So what I mean by that
is doing certain things

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.070
that will help bring down the cortisol

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.240
and norepinephrine levels

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.740
that are stirred up by anxiety,
and that might be something

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.120
like exercise, meditation, yoga.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.940
If normally you go to a
gym or an exercise class

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:01.907
or a yoga class,

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:05.470
this might be a time if it
feels more serving for you

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.753
to find an app and do something from home.

00:04:07.753 --> 00:04:10.549
A certain degree of self-care
is about distraction

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:13.105
and that's oftentimes
what we label as coping.

00:04:13.105 --> 00:04:16.920
But really we can't be having
this conversation in our heads

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.190
and with others 24/7

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.700
because this is debilitating.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.880
Listen to some podcasts

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.370
that are on a completely different topic

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.442
or watch a show or something.

00:04:24.442 --> 00:04:26.200
Try to do something that's engaging.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.035
Try to be present with people
that you feel safe around.

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:33.010
Most importantly too is
still finding connection

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.820
while we're practicing
the social distancing.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.290
So that might not look the same

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:40.110
as it would have a couple of months ago

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.199
but hey, this may be a time

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:44.526
to resurrect the phone conversation.

00:04:44.526 --> 00:04:47.570
Given the severity of this,
how are you helping clients

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.048
put some of this into perspective,

00:04:49.048 --> 00:04:51.730
and their anxiety around
it in perspective?

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:54.410
Some degree of anxiety
around this is helpful,

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.279
so I really do want to encourage people

00:04:56.279 --> 00:04:58.320
to not judge themselves for that anxiety

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.731
and to allow room to examine that anxiety

00:05:01.731 --> 00:05:04.510
and see what it's telling you.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.485
It can be helpful for people to remember

00:05:06.485 --> 00:05:09.198
do what is within your control

00:05:09.198 --> 00:05:13.050
and also still be able to live their life

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:16.717
to a certain extent.
Just make some changes.

